SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Kementerian Kesehatan Rilis Hasil Survei Status Gizi Indonesia (SSGI) tahun 2022","publisher":"Kementerian Kesehatan Republik Indonesia / UPK","publication_date":"2023-01-25","url":"https://upk.kemkes.go.id/new/kementerian-kesehatan-rilis-hasil-survei-status-gizi-indonesia-ssgi-tahun-2022","claim":"Indonesia’s stunting rate fell by 2.8 percentage points from 2021 to 2022, and the ministry linked the decline to interventions for adolescents, pregnant women, and children under 23 months, including ANC, iron tablets, complementary feeding, growth monitoring, exclusive breastfeeding, and more immunization.","locator":"lines 66-89","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry summary of the 2022 SSGI with specific intervention pathways and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the target year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Jokowi, through the health ministry and national stunting strategy.","contrary_evidence":["This is a ministry summary rather than independent validation.","The target of 14 percent by 2024 had not yet been reached."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Recovering from the impacts of COVID-19 school closures in Indonesia","publisher":"World Bank Blogs","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://blogs.worldbank.org/en/eastasiapacific/recovering-impacts-covid-19-school-closures-indonesia","claim":"The World Bank said school closures affected 68.8 million students and caused estimated learning loss of 0.9 to 1.2 years; after technical discussions with Indonesia’s education and religious ministries, it said the government and schools made strides through simplified curricula, teacher development, after-school tutoring, EdTech, and psychosocial support.","locator":"lines 42-66","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank analyst blog with concrete estimates and named ministry interactions, but not a formal evaluation report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 recovery context with retrospective learning-loss estimates.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Jokowi’s administration; the interventions were implemented through education and religious-ministry channels.","contrary_evidence":["The page says learning losses remained substantial.","It is a blog summary, not a case-by-case implementation audit."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on children’s learning in Indonesia","publisher":"UNICEF Indonesia","publication_date":"2022-03","url":"https://www.unicef.org/indonesia/reports/impact-covid-19-pandemic-childrens-learning-indonesia","claim":"The pandemic had a negative impact on learning across all education levels in Indonesia, and the brief focused on out-of-school children and those at risk of dropping out from socio-economically disadvantaged families.","locator":"lines 64-64","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent UNICEF issue brief centered on the education risks facing vulnerable groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 context for learning and youth welfare.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly contextual rather than a direct ruler action, but it defines the inherited learning-loss burden faced by Jokowi’s administration.","contrary_evidence":["This is a context brief rather than a policy evaluation.","It documents harm and risk, not delivery success."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- Reviewed roughly 25-30 documents across World Bank, WHO, UNICEF, BPS, Kemenkeu, Kemenkes, Setkab, Reuters/World Bank blog material, and a few broader background pages.
- Kept the strongest 2022 ruler-period sources that added distinct claims on health, social protection, stunting, learning recovery, and social-registry targeting; near-duplicate republications and broad background pages were dropped.
- Rejected or downgraded out-of-period material when it only repeated 2022 facts without adding ruler-specific detail, and skipped a 403-blocked Bappenas page that could not be cited reliably.
- Used the prior chapter’s BPS and World Bank evidence as reusable context for poverty, inequality, and human-capital trajectory instead of re-fetching equivalent structured series.

**Remaining gaps**
- Education quality is still thinner than health and social protection: the evidence shows large learning losses and recovery efforts, but not a full 2022 implementation audit by province or district.
- Beneficiary-level impact remains less certain than rollout and budget data for BLT BBM, BSU, Regsosek, immunization, and stunting programs.
- Distributional detail for eastern provinces, disability, and gender-specific outcomes is present in survey framing, but still not as complete as a dedicated 2022 household-welfare dataset.
